 Today's newsletter is 839 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📉 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down 1%, finishing at its lowest mark since late July.

 🥶 Today's stock spotlight: Salesforce (-9.4%) and other software stocks fell in the run-up to Anthropic's IPO.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Nvidia speaks 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo: Cheng Xin/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nvidia posted a stronger-than-expected third quarter and a bullish outlook in its first earnings report after Jensen Huang was ousted as CEO in a scandal that has reportedly spawned an SEC investigation.

 💸 Zoom in: Nvidia recorded quarterly revenue of $115.8 billion and net income of $65.3 billion, both more than doubling its performance from a year earlier.

 S&amp;P Capital IQ analysts had expected revenue of $108.7 billion and net income of $60.5 billion. The company's data center revenue — its largest segment — rose 110% from a year ago to $107.6 billion. The showing — which included a whopping gross profit margin of 74.8% — immediately calmed investors who have shaved more than $1 trillion off of Nvidia's market cap since the Huang scandal broke.

 The stock was up more than 1% in after-hours trading. 🔍 What we're watching: It marks the first moment for interim CEO Colette Kress — who is also still serving as CFO — to exert her leadership during the most tumultuous moment in the company's history.

 She didn't mention Huang in the earnings report but touted Nvidia's role as "an exceptional technology leader" and said sales of the company's new, advanced Vera Rubin chips are "off to an explosive start." "Our roadmap is on track and our ambition has never been greater," she said. 🕵 Friction point: WSJ reported this afternoon that the SEC has opened a civil probe into Nvidia.

 An SEC spokesperson declined to comment. Nvidia reps did not immediately respond to a request for comment. What's next: Tech analyst Gene Munster said on X that Nvidia's earnings call today is "the most anticipated conference call of all time, and the one we'll likely get the least information from."

