 2. Jensen applies for the job 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Jensen Huang in July. Photo: Tomohiro Ohsumi/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nvidia has a job opening for a permanent CEO, and recently ousted Jensen Huang today officially threw his hat in the ring.

 🗣️ "Since my departure, I've seen an outpouring of calls from Nvidia's employees, investors, business partners, and customers for me to return as CEO," Huang's statement said, released hours before the company's third quarter earnings call. "If the Board of Directors decides that my return is in the best interest of our shareholders, our employees, the company, and America, I would be honored to answer these calls and return as CEO." Reality check: Besides the reported SEC probe, Nvidia is conducting its own investigation into Huang's conduct, and lawmakers said today he's agreed to testify before Congress next month, Axios' Herb Scribner writes .

 Newly appointed Nvidia chairman Harvey Jones said on this afternoon's earnings call that the board unanimously accepted Huang's resignation "and does not intend to reinstate Jensen as CEO." Catch up quick: Huang resigned last week after reports of alleged affairs with two Nvidia employees and former Megaspeed CEO Alice Huang, who is suspected of being a Chinese spy.

 🔍 What we're watching: Lawmakers want answers on whether Nvidia's powerful AI chips were compromised by Chinese intelligence and whether the company did enough to protect sensitive American technology.

