 4. 🕔 After hours: Kalshi&#x27;s forbidden words 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour speaks at Axios BFD on Tuesday. Photo: Sam Popp for Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Kalshi took down markets allowing traders to bet on the words President Trump will say in public — but that doesn't mean it wanted to.

 🗣️ Driving the news: Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour said at Axios BFD today that the prediction market removed the so-called mention markets after the Commodity Futures Trading Commission asked it to.

 "We disagree with this decision," Mansour told Axios' Madison Mills on stage. "What the president does say moves markets. So predicting what he's about to say is actually pretty meaningful." 🥊 Friction point: The CFTC said in its order that "Kalshi has not attempted to establish that the Contracts are non-manipulable."

 Mansour argued that Kalshi has implemented "significant guardrails" and "did catch a few people that did try to move these markets." 💸 Flashback: The CFTC launched an investigation earlier this year on whether a White House teleprompter operator capitalized on his knowledge of Trump's prepared speech text by making trades on Kalshi.

